\section{Note on the coding convention}{

The survival convention is used: \code{1} means the unit is \emph{censored} (drops out of observation) and \code{0} means it remains under observation. This is the opposite of an "observed" or "event" indicator. When a censoring model is supplied to \code{\link[=weightit]{weightit()}}, the returned propensity score is \eqn{P(C = 1 | X)}, the probability of \emph{being censored}.
}

\section{Assessing balance}{

\code{cobalt::bal.tab()} cannot be used directly on the output of a censoring model. For a point-treatment \code{weightit} object it errors because every censored unit has a weight of 0, leaving one "treatment group" with no weight; for a \code{weightitMSM} object it errors because censoring leaves missing values in the later treatments, which \code{bal.tab()} does not allow.

Because the target of a censoring model is the full at-risk sample rather than another treatment group, balance is assessed by comparing the weighted uncensored units against that sample. This can be done by stacking the two:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{u <- which(W$treat == 0)
bal.tab(rbind(covs[u, ], covs),
        treat = c(rep(0L, length(u)), rep(1L, nrow(covs))),
        weights = c(W$weights[u], rep(1, nrow(covs))),
        estimand = "ATT", s.d.denom = "treated")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The "Control" group is then the weighted uncensored sample and the "Treated" group the full at-risk sample, so \code{Diff.Adj} is the quantity the weights are designed to zero out. For a \code{weightitMSM} object, use the \code{at.risk} component, which has one column per time point, to restrict each model to the units that were under observation when it was fit:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{ar <- W$at.risk[, "A_3"]
bal.tab(A_3 ~ X1_2 + X2_2 + A_2, data = data[ar, ],
        weights = W$weights[ar], s.weights = W$s.weights[ar])
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\examples{
data("msmdata")

# A censoring indicator: 1 = lost to follow-up after time 2
set.seed(1234)
msmdata$C_2 <- rbinom(nrow(msmdata), 1,
                      prob = plogis(-2 + 0.2 * msmdata$X1_1))

# Inverse probability of censoring weights
(Wc <- weightit(.cens(C_2) ~ X1_1 + X2_1 + A_1,
                data = msmdata, method = "glm"))

# Censored units have a weight of 0
all(Wc$weights[msmdata$C_2 == 1] == 0)

# The weights are the inverse probability of remaining
# under observation
all.equal(Wc$weights[msmdata$C_2 == 0],
          1 / (1 - Wc$ps[msmdata$C_2 == 0]))

# `.cens()` can also tag a variable directly for use with
# the lower-level interfaces
C <- .cens(msmdata$C_2)

all.equal(get_w_from_ps(Wc$ps, treat = C),
          Wc$weights, check.attributes = FALSE)
}
